<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">South Hsenwi</h1><p class="st-meta">1888 CE – 1959 CE · capital Mong Yai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern half of the old Shan state of Hsenwi, created by the British in 1888 and governed from Mong Yai until 1959.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the British annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, the large and war-torn Shan state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a> was partitioned in 1888 to settle the civil war between rival claimants. Its southern part became South Hsenwi, with its capital at Mong Yai, under one of the contending families.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a> was ruled by a hereditary saopha under British supervision, in a region of Shan farmers and hill peoples on the routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a> and the plateau. Like other Shan states it collected its own revenues and administered customary law, paying tribute to the British government. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a> in 1922.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Burmese independence in 1948 South <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Union of Burma</a>. Its saopha, like the other Shan rulers, surrendered his powers to the state government in 1959. Mong Yai remains a town in northern Shan State.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/south-hsenwi.jpg" alt="South Hsenwi" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: User:Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan_States-Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1888 CE – 1959 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mong Yai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hsenwi (South), Mong Yai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1924&amp;focus=south-hsenwi" aria-label="South Hsenwi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7304 1351 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where South Hsenwi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7624 1607L7612 1587M7624 1607L7616 1809M7624 1607L7611 1665"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7612 1587h0M7616 1809h0M7611 1665h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7624 1607h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7624 1607h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1924&amp;focus=south-hsenwi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
